1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,967
Imagine a small office with several computers.
When one computer sends

2
00:00:04,967 --> 00:00:09,933
a message, how do the others know who it is
for?

3
00:00:09,933 --> 00:00:14,900
Each needs an address, a little like a house
number. Here

4
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:19,867
we use IPv4 addresses, written as four numbers
with dots between

5
00:00:19,867 --> 00:00:24,833
them. Watch a message go to the computer
marked 192.168.1.20. Our

6
00:00:24,833 --> 00:00:29,800
question today is how to divide one collection
of these addresses

7
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:34,767
into smaller groups. That division is called
subnetting.

8
00:00:34,767 --> 00:00:39,862
Before dividing anything, meet the smallest
building block: a bit. A

9
00:00:39,862 --> 00:00:44,957
bit can be zero or one. Think of a switch with

10
00:00:44,957 --> 00:00:50,052
just two positions. Put eight bits side by
side and they

11
00:00:50,052 --> 00:00:55,148
can represent one of the numbers in our
address. All four

12
00:00:55,148 --> 00:01:00,243
numbers together use thirty-two bits. The dots
make the address easier

13
00:01:00,243 --> 00:01:05,338
for people to read; underneath, it is a row of
zeros

14
00:01:05,338 --> 00:01:10,433
and ones. We will mostly watch the final eight
positions.

15
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:15,462
Look at these addresses: they all start with
192.168.1, while the

16
00:01:15,462 --> 00:01:20,490
last number changes. Those first three numbers
use twenty-four bits. If

17
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:25,519
we keep those bits fixed, we get one group
with eight

18
00:01:25,519 --> 00:01:30,548
positions left to change. We write the group
as 192.168.1.0 slash

19
00:01:30,548 --> 00:01:35,576
twenty-four. The slash tells us how many
beginning bits stay fixed.

20
00:01:35,576 --> 00:01:40,605
That shared beginning is called a prefix. It
is simply the

21
00:01:40,605 --> 00:01:45,633
part every address in this group has in
common.

22
00:01:45,633 --> 00:01:50,200
One free bit has two choices: zero or one. Two
free

23
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:54,767
bits have four: zero-zero, zero-one, one-zero,
one-one. Every extra free bit

24
00:01:54,767 --> 00:01:59,333
doubles the choices. Keep doubling until eight
bits give two hundred

25
00:01:59,333 --> 00:02:03,900
and fifty-six possibilities. We write that as
two to the power

26
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:08,467
of eight. The final number can therefore run
from zero through

27
00:02:08,467 --> 00:02:13,033
two hundred and fifty-five. Zero counts as a
possibility too. Now

28
00:02:13,033 --> 00:02:17,600
we know the size of the group we are about to

29
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:22,167
divide.

30
00:02:22,167 --> 00:02:26,733
Take the first of those eight free bits and
use it

31
00:02:26,733 --> 00:02:31,300
to choose a side. Zero means the left half;
one means

32
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:35,867
the right half. Seven bits are still free
inside each half,

33
00:02:35,867 --> 00:02:40,433
giving one hundred and twenty-eight addresses
apiece. Each smaller group now

34
00:02:40,433 --> 00:02:45,000
shares twenty-five beginning bits, so we call
it slash twenty-five. Nothing

35
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,567
new has been added. The same addresses have
been sorted into

36
00:02:49,567 --> 00:02:54,133
two groups, just as one drawer can be divided
into two

37
00:02:54,133 --> 00:02:58,700
compartments.

38
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:03,795
Use one more bit to split each half. Now the
group

39
00:03:03,795 --> 00:03:08,890
labels are zero-zero, zero-one, one-zero, and
one-one. Two choices followed by

40
00:03:08,890 --> 00:03:13,986
two choices make four groups. These smaller
address groups are our

41
00:03:13,986 --> 00:03:19,081
subnets. Each shares twenty-six beginning
bits, so each is slash twenty-six.

42
00:03:19,081 --> 00:03:24,176
This is why moving the boundary just two
places turns one

43
00:03:24,176 --> 00:03:29,271
network's address block into four smaller
blocks. The labels may look

44
00:03:29,271 --> 00:03:34,367
technical, but underneath we have only made
two yes-or-no choices.

45
00:03:34,367 --> 00:03:39,395
We started with eight free positions and used
two to choose

46
00:03:39,395 --> 00:03:44,424
a group. That leaves six positions to choose
addresses inside each

47
00:03:44,424 --> 00:03:49,452
group. These are called host bits; a host is a
device

48
00:03:49,452 --> 00:03:54,481
using an address. Six free bits give sixty-
four combinations. Check our

49
00:03:54,481 --> 00:03:59,510
work: four groups times sixty-four addresses
equals two hundred and fifty-six,

50
00:03:59,510 --> 00:04:04,538
exactly where we began. A longer shared
beginning makes each individual

51
00:04:04,538 --> 00:04:09,567
group smaller, because fewer bits remain free
to change.

52
00:04:09,567 --> 00:04:14,192
Put zeros in all six positions inside each
group. That gives

53
00:04:14,192 --> 00:04:18,817
its first address. Change only the two group
bits and watch

54
00:04:18,817 --> 00:04:23,442
the starts appear: zero, sixty-four, one
hundred and twenty-eight, one hundred

55
00:04:23,442 --> 00:04:28,067
and ninety-two. We only show the last number
here; 192.168.1 stays

56
00:04:28,067 --> 00:04:32,692
the same throughout. Why do the starts jump by
sixty-four? Each

57
00:04:32,692 --> 00:04:37,317
group occupies sixty-four places. The next
group starts immediately after those

58
00:04:37,317 --> 00:04:41,942
places finish. We can count the spaces instead
of memorizing four

59
00:04:41,942 --> 00:04:46,567
special numbers.

60
00:04:46,567 --> 00:04:51,192
A computer needs a way to mark which bits
belong to

61
00:04:51,192 --> 00:04:55,817
the shared beginning. We use a mask, like a
stencil: ones

62
00:04:55,817 --> 00:05:00,442
mark the shared positions, zeros mark the
changing positions. Our first

63
00:05:00,442 --> 00:05:05,067
twenty-four bits are shared, plus two more in
the final group

64
00:05:05,067 --> 00:05:09,692
of eight. That last pattern is one-one
followed by six zeros.

65
00:05:09,692 --> 00:05:14,317
The first two positions are worth one hundred
and twenty-eight and

66
00:05:14,317 --> 00:05:18,942
sixty-four. Add them to get one hundred and
ninety-two. Our mask

67
00:05:18,942 --> 00:05:23,567
is 255.255.255.192.

68
00:05:23,567 --> 00:05:28,533
Try the address ending in seventy. In bits,
seventy is zero-one-zero-zero-zero-one-one-
zero.

69
00:05:28,533 --> 00:05:33,500
Place our mask underneath it. Keep an address
bit where the

70
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:38,467
mask has a one; replace it with zero where the
mask

71
00:05:38,467 --> 00:05:43,433
has a zero. This operation is called AND. The
answer is

72
00:05:43,433 --> 00:05:48,400
zero-one followed by six zeros: sixty-four. So
seventy belongs to the

73
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:53,367
group starting at sixty-four. The mask has
erased the individual device's

74
00:05:53,367 --> 00:05:58,333
part and left the group's starting address
behind.

75
00:05:58,333 --> 00:06:02,958
Can we give all sixty-four addresses to
computers? In this ordinary

76
00:06:02,958 --> 00:06:07,583
local network example, two have other jobs.
The first address names

77
00:06:07,583 --> 00:06:12,208
the whole subnet. The last is the broadcast
address, used for

78
00:06:12,208 --> 00:06:16,833
a message addressed to everyone in that
subnet. That leaves sixty-two

79
00:06:16,833 --> 00:06:21,458
device addresses: sixty-four minus two.
Remember what we counted before: total

80
00:06:21,458 --> 00:06:26,083
addresses, not just device addresses. Some
special network arrangements use different

81
00:06:26,083 --> 00:06:30,708
rules, so we are applying this subtraction to
our ordinary local

82
00:06:30,708 --> 00:06:35,333
network example.

83
00:06:35,333 --> 00:06:39,900
The first group runs from zero to sixty-three.
Reserve zero for

84
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:44,467
the network and sixty-three for broadcast.
Computers use one through sixty-two.

85
00:06:44,467 --> 00:06:49,033
The next group runs from sixty-four to one
hundred and twenty-seven.

86
00:06:49,033 --> 00:06:53,600
Reserve those endpoints, leaving sixty-five
through one hundred and twenty-six for

87
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:58,167
computers. Follow the colored ends of each bar
as we name

88
00:06:58,167 --> 00:07:02,733
their jobs. Broadcast does not always end in
two hundred and

89
00:07:02,733 --> 00:07:07,300
fifty-five. It is the final address of the
particular subnet we

90
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:11,867
chose.

91
00:07:11,867 --> 00:07:16,492
The third group starts at one hundred and
twenty-eight and ends

92
00:07:16,492 --> 00:07:21,117
at one hundred and ninety-one. Its device
addresses sit between those

93
00:07:21,117 --> 00:07:25,742
endpoints. The fourth starts at one hundred
and ninety-two and ends

94
00:07:25,742 --> 00:07:30,367
at two hundred and fifty-five, again with the
middle addresses available

95
00:07:30,367 --> 00:07:34,992
for devices. Look across all four bars. They
fit together without

96
00:07:34,992 --> 00:07:39,617
gaps or overlaps. Every address from our
original group has a

97
00:07:39,617 --> 00:07:44,242
place, and each smaller group follows the same
rule for its

98
00:07:44,242 --> 00:07:48,867
two endpoints.

99
00:07:48,867 --> 00:07:54,024
Suppose one group needs fifty computers. How
much room should we

100
00:07:54,024 --> 00:07:59,181
leave? Five free bits give thirty-two total
addresses. After the two

101
00:07:59,181 --> 00:08:04,338
special ones, only thirty remain. Too small.
Six free bits give

102
00:08:04,338 --> 00:08:09,495
sixty-four total and sixty-two for devices.
That fits. An IPv4 address

103
00:08:09,495 --> 00:08:14,652
has thirty-two bits, so leaving six free means
fixing twenty-six: slash

104
00:08:14,652 --> 00:08:19,810
twenty-six. We chose the size by counting the
room needed. The

105
00:08:19,810 --> 00:08:24,967
slash number followed from that choice, rather
than appearing from nowhere.

106
00:08:24,967 --> 00:08:29,533
Do addresses ending in seventy and one hundred
belong together? Our

107
00:08:29,533 --> 00:08:34,100
mask sends both to the group starting at
sixty-four, so yes.

108
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:38,667
What about seventy and one hundred and thirty?
The second one

109
00:08:38,667 --> 00:08:43,233
belongs to the group starting at one hundred
and twenty-eight. They

110
00:08:43,233 --> 00:08:47,800
are in different subnets. This is why the mask
matters alongside

111
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:52,367
the address. Seeing the same first three
dotted numbers is not

112
00:08:52,367 --> 00:08:56,933
enough: our smaller groups share two more bits
after those first

113
00:08:56,933 --> 00:09:01,500
twenty-four.

114
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:06,125
We started with computers needing addresses.
Those addresses became rows of

115
00:09:06,125 --> 00:09:10,750
bits, and bits gave us simple choices. Keep a
beginning fixed,

116
00:09:10,750 --> 00:09:15,375
then count the endings that can change. Using
two more bits

117
00:09:15,375 --> 00:09:20,000
for group labels made four subnets, each with
sixty-four addresses. The

118
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,625
starts, the mask, and the device counts all
came from that

119
00:09:24,625 --> 00:09:29,250
one division. Subnetting looks like a page of
mysterious numbers until

120
00:09:29,250 --> 00:09:33,875
you see the picture underneath: one
collection, a movable boundary, and

121
00:09:33,875 --> 00:09:38,500
smaller groups.
